var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Vensim-functions","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Each Vensim function: what it is for, its definition, and how to write it in PySD.jl (what the translator writes).  They fall into groups by how they behave in time, which decides how they run with other solvers than Vensim's Euler steps (Solvers and the model's ticks):\n\nMemory as a stock: hidden stocks with rates.  Any solver integrates them.\nMemory by counting steps: values at earlier steps of Δt.  They move at the model's ticks, whatever the solver.\nSwitches at given times: functions of the time.  The solver stops at the ticks, so a switch at a tick is exact.\nSwitches on values, and plain calculations: no time behaviour of their own.\n\nIn the definitions, t_k = t_0 + kΔt are the ticks.","title":"Vensim functions"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Memory-as-a-stock","page":"Vensim functions","text":"","title":"Memory as a stock"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#SMOOTH,-SMOOTHI","page":"Vensim functions","text":"SMOOTH(input, T) · SMOOTHI(input, T, initial_value)\n\nPerception and adjustment: what people believe about a quantity lags behind its real value (perceived demand, an expected price, the average of a noisy series).  Each unit of time the output closes a share 1/T of its gap to the input: after a jump, 63 % of the way in T, 95 % in 3T.\n\nS(t_0) = textinput(t_0)  (textSMOOTHI initial_value) qquad\nfracdSdt = fractextinput - ST qquad textSMOOTH = S\n\nPySD.jl: smooth(input, T), smooth(input, T; init = initial_value).","title":"SMOOTH, SMOOTHI"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#SMOOTH3,-SMOOTH3I,-SMOOTH-N","page":"Vensim functions","text":"SMOOTH3(input, T) · SMOOTH3I(input, T, initial_value) · SMOOTH N(input, T, initial_value, n)\n\nThe same through a chain of n smooths, each of time T/n: the output hardly reacts at first, then catches up (an S-shape).  Information that goes through several stages: measured, reported, then acted on.\n\nfracdS_1dt = (textinput - S_1)fracnT qquad\nfracdS_idt = (S_i-1 - S_i)fracnT qquad textoutput = S_n\n\nwith every S_i(t_0) the input's (or initial_value).\n\nPySD.jl: smooth(input, T; order = 3), smooth(input, T; order = n, init = initial_value).","title":"SMOOTH3, SMOOTH3I, SMOOTH N"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#DELAY1,-DELAY1I","page":"Vensim functions","text":"DELAY1(input, T) · DELAY1I(input, T, initial_value)\n\nMaterial in transit: goods being shipped, buildings under construction, people in training.  Whatever goes in comes out later and nothing is lost; things stay T on average, some leaving early and some late.\n\nL(t_0) = textinput(t_0)T qquad fracdLdt = textinput - fracLT qquad\ntextDELAY1 = fracLT\n\nWith a constant T this is the curve of SMOOTH.  They differ when T changes: a delay keeps its contents L, so its outflow changes at once; a smooth keeps its output.\n\nPySD.jl: delay(input, T), delay(input, T; init = initial_value).","title":"DELAY1, DELAY1I"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#DELAY3,-DELAY3I,-DELAY-N","page":"Vensim functions","text":"DELAY3(input, T) · DELAY3I(input, T, initial_value) · DELAY N(input, T, initial_value, n)\n\nMaterial through n stages in a row, each of T/n: most of it comes out around T after it went in (construction, ageing chains).  The larger n, the closer to a fixed delay.\n\nfracdL_1dt = textinput - L_1fracnT qquad\nfracdL_idt = (L_i-1 - L_i)fracnT qquad\ntextoutput = L_nfracnT\n\nwith L_i(t_0) = textinput(t_0)Tn (or initial_value·T/n).\n\nPySD.jl: delay(input, T; order = 3), delay(input, T; order = n, init = initial_value).","title":"DELAY3, DELAY3I, DELAY N"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#TREND","page":"Vensim functions","text":"TREND(input, T, initial_trend)\n\nThe growth rate someone watching the input perceives, as a fraction per unit of time (\"sales grow 5 % a year\"): the input against its smoothed past.\n\nA(t_0) = fractextinput(t_0)1 + textinitial_trendcdot T qquad\nfracdAdt = fractextinput - AT qquad\ntextTREND = fractextinput - ATA  (0 text if  A = 0)\n\nFor an input growing steadily at a rate g, TREND settles on g.\n\nPySD.jl: trend(input, T; init = initial_trend).","title":"TREND"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#FORECAST","page":"Vensim functions","text":"FORECAST(input, T, horizon)\n\nThe input's perceived trend extended a horizon ahead, to plan capacity or investment.\n\nfracdAdt = fractextinput - AT qquad\ntextFORECAST = textinputleft(1 + texthorizonfractextinput - ATAright)\n\nwith A(t_0) = textinput(t_0)  (1 + textinitial trend), as Python PySD.\n\nPySD.jl: the translator writes it with a smooth for A: input * (1 + zidz(input - A, T * A) * horizon), A = smooth(input, T; init = input / (1 + initial_trend)).","title":"FORECAST"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#INITIAL","page":"Vensim functions","text":"INITIAL(value)\n\nA value frozen at the start of the run: a reference level (\"population in the base year\"), or a starting value computed from other variables.\n\ntextINITIAL(x)(t) = x(t_0) quad textfor every  t\n\nPySD.jl: x(t₀).","title":"INITIAL"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Memory-by-counting-steps","page":"Vensim functions","text":"These move at the model's ticks, with any solver: the solver stops at every tick, where they record and move, and holds them in between.","title":"Memory by counting steps"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#DELAY-FIXED","page":"Vensim functions","text":"DELAY FIXED(input, T, initial_value)\n\nAn exact pipeline: everything comes out exactly T later, unchanged.  A fixed construction or maturing time, a legal waiting period: a conveyor belt rather than a mixing tank.\n\nn = maxleft(1 operatornameroundfracTΔtright) qquad\ntextoutput(t_k) = begincases textinitial_value  k  n  textinput(t_k-n)  k ge n endcases\n\nn is fixed when the model is built: changing T needs a rebuild.\n\nPySD.jl: input(t - T; init = initial_value).","title":"DELAY FIXED"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#SAMPLE-IF-TRUE","page":"Vensim functions","text":"SAMPLE IF TRUE(condition, input, initial_value)\n\nFollows the input while a condition holds and keeps the last value when it stops: a value fixed at a given year, the price at the last review.\n\ntextoutput(t_k) = begincases textinput(t_k)  textcondition(t_k) \ntextoutput(t_k-1)  textotherwise endcases\n\nand initial_value until the condition first holds.  A condition such as t == 2020 is read at the ticks, so it holds there with any solver.\n\nPySD.jl: sample_if_true(condition, input; init = initial_value).","title":"SAMPLE IF TRUE"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#RANDOM-0-1,-RANDOM-UNIFORM,-RANDOM-NORMAL,-RANDOM-EXPONENTIAL","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Noise: demand fluctuations, measurement errors, Monte Carlo experiments. Vensim draws one number per step and keeps it for the step; the seed makes a run repeatable.\n\nAs Python PySD: RANDOM UNIFORM is uniform on [min, max]; RANDOM NORMAL(min, max, mean, sd, seed) a normal truncated to [min, max] (drawn again while outside); RANDOM EXPONENTIAL(min, max, shift, stretch, seed) is shift + stretch times an exponential, from at least min, truncated at max.\n\nPySD.jl: random_0_1(), random_uniform(min, max; seed), random_normal(min, max, mean, sd; seed), random_exponential(min, max, shift, stretch; seed); dims = d for a draw per element of d where the arguments are numbers.  Drawn once per tick from a generator seeded from the seed, the call, the element and the tick: held between ticks, and the same in every run, with any solver.","title":"RANDOM 0 1, RANDOM UNIFORM, RANDOM NORMAL, RANDOM EXPONENTIAL"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Switches-at-given-times","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Functions of the time.  A model with them stops at every tick, and a solver's step that ends at a tick sees the time just before it, so a switch at a tick changes the rates from that tick on.","title":"Switches at given times"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#PULSE","page":"Vensim functions","text":"PULSE(start, width): a one-off event of a given length (a two-year subsidy, a test shock).\n\ntextPULSE = begincases 1  textstart le t  textstart + textwidth  0  textotherwise endcases\n\nPySD.jl: (start ≤ t < start + width).","title":"PULSE"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#PULSE-TRAIN","page":"Vensim functions","text":"PULSE TRAIN(start, width, interval, end): a repeating event (a yearly harvest, periodic maintenance).\n\ntextPULSE TRAIN = begincases 1  textstart le t  textend text and  (t - textstart) bmod textinterval  textwidth  0  textotherwise endcases\n\nPySD.jl: pysd_pulse_train(t, start, interval, width, end).","title":"PULSE TRAIN"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#STEP","page":"Vensim functions","text":"STEP(height, step_time): a permanent change from a given time (a new tax rate, a policy switch); also the standard test input.\n\ntextSTEP = begincases textheight  t ge textstep_time  0  textbefore endcases\n\nPySD.jl: height * (t ≥ step_time).","title":"STEP"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#RAMP","page":"Vensim functions","text":"RAMP(slope, start, end): a gradual change between two times (a phase-in).\n\ntextRAMP = textslope cdot operatornameclamp(t - textstart 0 textend - textstart)\n\nPySD.jl: slope * clamp(t - start, 0, finish - start).","title":"RAMP"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Data-series","page":"Vensim functions","text":"GET XLS DATA, GET DIRECT DATA, and data variables: historical series or scenarios read from a spreadsheet or file.  Between two data points, a straight line (or the value kept, looking forward, or the raw values); before the first and after the last, held.\n\nPySD.jl: data(sheet, time, cells; method = :interpolate) as a parameter, called as series(t); data(parents, :name) for a series from another model's run or a file (Data from other models or from files).","title":"Data series"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Switches-on-values,-limits-and-choices","page":"Vensim functions","text":"","title":"Switches on values, limits and choices"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#IF-THEN-ELSE","page":"Vensim functions","text":"IF THEN ELSE(condition, a, b): rules that switch (order when the stock is below target, start a policy once a threshold is passed).  a if the condition holds, b otherwise.\n\nPySD.jl: ifelse(condition, a, b); ifelse.(…) for arrays.","title":"IF THEN ELSE"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#MIN,-MAX","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Physical limits: shipments can't exceed what is in stock, a flow can't go below zero (MAX(0, x)).\n\nPySD.jl: min(a, b), max(a, b).","title":"MIN, MAX"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#INTEGER,-QUANTUM,-MODULO","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Whole units (power plants, people), rounding to a batch size, cycles such as the month within a year.\n\ntextINTEGER(x) = operatornametrunc(x) qquad\ntextQUANTUM(x q) = qoperatornametrunc(x  q) qquad\ntextMODULO(x m) = x - mleftlfloor fracxm rightrfloor\n\nPySD.jl: trunc(x), pysd_quantum(x, q), mod(x, m).","title":"INTEGER, QUANTUM, MODULO"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#VECTOR-SELECT,-VECTOR-RANK,-VECTOR-SORT-ORDER,-VECTOR-REORDER","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Working along a dimension: rank technologies by cost, pick the cheapest, add up only the selected elements.\n\nPySD.jl: not translated yet (helpers for the older backends: pysd_vector_select and the pysd_vector_* functions).","title":"VECTOR SELECT, VECTOR RANK, VECTOR SORT ORDER, VECTOR REORDER"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#ALLOCATE-BY-PRIORITY,-ALLOCATE-AVAILABLE","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Sharing a limited supply among users who each ask for an amount, the highest priority served first.\n\nPySD.jl: not translated yet (helpers for the older backends: pysd_allocate_by_priority, pysd_allocate_available).","title":"ALLOCATE BY PRIORITY, ALLOCATE AVAILABLE"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Plain-calculations","page":"Vensim functions","text":"","title":"Plain calculations"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#XIDZ,-ZIDZ","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Divisions that may hit zero (a share of a total that can be empty).\n\ntextXIDZ(a b x) = begincases a  b  b ne 0  x  b = 0 endcases qquad\ntextZIDZ(a b) = textXIDZ(a b 0)\n\nPySD.jl: xidz(a, b, x), zidz(a, b).","title":"XIDZ, ZIDZ"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#SUM,-PROD,-VMIN,-VMAX,-ELMCOUNT","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Collapsing a dimension (the ! in Vensim): a world total from regions, the most expensive technology, the number of regions.\n\nPySD.jl: sum_over(x, d), prod_over(x, d), minimum_over(x, d), maximum_over(x, d) (several dimensions: sum_over(x, (d1, d2))); length(d).","title":"SUM, PROD, VMIN, VMAX, ELMCOUNT"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Lookups","page":"Vensim functions","text":"A relationship known as a curve or from data (the effect of price on demand); its argument can be any variable.  Between two points, a straight line; outside them, held at the first or last value.\n\nPySD.jl: piecewise_linear(x₁ => y₁, x₂ => y₂, …) (WITH LOOKUP and lookups written in the model), lookup(sheet, x, cells) (GET XLS LOOKUPS, GET DIRECT LOOKUPS); called as f(x).","title":"Lookups"},{"category":"section","location":"functions/#Maths-and-the-rest","page":"Vensim functions","text":"Vensim PySD.jl\n+ - * / ^, ABS, SQRT, EXP, LN + - * / ^, abs, sqrt, exp, log\nLOG(x, b) log(x) / log(b)\nSIN, COS, TAN, ARCSIN, ARCCOS, ARCTAN, SINH, COSH, TANH sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, sinh, cosh, tanh\nPI π\n= <> < <= > >=, :AND:, :OR:, :NOT: == != < <= > >=, &, |, !\nINVERT MATRIX(A) inv(A)\nGET XLS CONSTANTS, GET DIRECT CONSTANTS constant(sheet, cells)\nACTIVE INITIAL(x, x0) (a whole equation) x with the initial value x0\nGET TIME VALUE(0, offset, 0) t + offset\nGAME not translated yet","title":"Maths and the rest"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#Sensitivity-and-derivatives","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"A model is compiled once, when it is built; every run after that, with any parameter values or input sheets, compiles nothing.  That is what a sensitivity analysis needs: many runs with different values.","title":"Sensitivity and derivatives"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#One-parameter-at-a-time","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"How much the final temperature of the teacup moves when each parameter is 1 % higher (its elasticity: the % change of the result for a 1 % change of the parameter):\n\nfinal(r) = r[:teacup_temperature][end]\nbase = run_model(m)\nfor p in (:room_temperature, :characteristic_time)\n    x = base[p]\n    r = run_model(m; params = NamedTuple{(p,)}((1.01 * x,)))\n    println(rpad(p, 22), round((final(r) - final(base)) / final(base) / 0.01; digits = 3))\nend\nroom_temperature      0.883\ncharacteristic_time   0.218\n\nThree things to watch in a large model:\n\nYears.  A parameter that is a year (policy_start = 2025) moves 20 years with 1 %.  Move it by one year instead.\nSwitches.  A parameter that chooses between options (scenario = 2) has no elasticity; run each option.\nStructure.  A parameter that sets the number of steps of a fixed delay or the order of a smooth can't change without building the model again; run_model says so.","title":"One parameter at a time"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#Many-runs","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"values = range(0.05, 0.15; length = 100)\nfinals = [run_model(m; params = (; k))[:x][end] for k in values]\n\nOnce compiled, a run of a large model takes about a second, so a sweep over every parameter of such a model takes minutes.  The runs are independent, so they can share the work between threads (start Julia with --threads=auto):\n\nfinals = fetch.([Threads.@spawn run_model(m; params = (; k))[:x][end] for k in values])\n\nEach run has its own buffers and histories; the input sheets they read are cached once for all.","title":"Many runs"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#Derivatives:-ForwardDiff","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"A run computes in the number type of its parameters, so other kinds of numbers go through it.  With ForwardDiff.jl, the derivative of any result in any parameter:\n\nusing ForwardDiff\nForwardDiff.derivative(R -> run_model(m; params = (; room_temperature = R))[:teacup_temperature][end], 70.0)\n0.9511454483920925\n\nIt is the exact derivative of the run, with its Euler steps, not an estimate from runs with nearby values; here, 1 − (1 − Δt/τ)²⁴⁰.  The first call compiles the model again for ForwardDiff's numbers; later ones don't.\n\nForwardDiff.gradient gives the derivatives in several parameters at once, for calibration with an optimiser.","title":"Derivatives: ForwardDiff"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#A-derivative-that-comes-out-NaN","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"A function whose derivative is infinite where the model takes it, such as sqrt(x) at x = 0, gives ∞ · 0 = NaN when x doesn't depend on the parameter, although the derivative is 0; the NaN then spreads to the stocks. ForwardDiff's NaN-safe mode counts ∞ · 0 as 0:\n\nusing Preferences, ForwardDiff\nset_preferences!(ForwardDiff, \"nansafe_mode\" => true)    # then start Julia again\n\nIt is saved in the environment's LocalPreferences.toml, and makes ForwardDiff a little slower.  On a large model where an abundance reached exactly zero, it turned NaN derivatives into ones that agree with central differences to 10⁻⁸.","title":"A derivative that comes out NaN"},{"category":"section","location":"sensitivity/#Uncertainties:-Measurements","page":"Sensitivity and derivatives","text":"With Measurements.jl, a parameter's uncertainty carried to every result:\n\nusing Measurements\nr = run_model(m; params = (; characteristic_time = 10 ± 1))\nr[:teacup_temperature][end]\n75.4 ± 1.6\n\nThis is first-order error propagation: right for small uncertainties.  For large ones, or to see the whole distribution, run the model for many values drawn at random (a Monte Carlo analysis, as above).","title":"Uncertainties: Measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"packages/#Models-as-packages","page":"Models as packages","text":"Building and compiling a large model takes minutes, every time a new Julia session starts.  To do that only once, keep the model in a package: Julia saves the built and compiled model when the package is first used, and later sessions load it ready to run.\n\nA model folder becomes a package with two more files:\n\nmymodel/\n├── Project.toml        # name = \"MyModel\", uuid, and PySD in [deps]\n├── src/MyModel.jl\n├── mymodel.jl          # the model, as before: it ends with Model(…)\n└── …                   # its other files, as before\n\n# src/MyModel.jl\nmodule MyModel\nusing PySD\n@precompile_model model = include(\"../mymodel.jl\")\nend\n\n[@precompile_model](@ref) definesmodel` (a constant), and builds and runs it once while Julia precompiles the package, so that all this compiles is saved with the package.\n\nThen:\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.develop(path = \"mymodel\")   # once, in the environment that has PySD.jl\nusing MyModel                              # later sessions: seconds\nr = run_model(MyModel.model; params = (; k = 0.2))\n\nThe first using MyModel builds and compiles the model and saves it; it takes as long as building it without a package, but only once.  For a model of 1,867 names: about 8 minutes the first time, then about 8 s to load it and 6 s for its first run in every later session (instead of about 9 minutes in each session).\nWhen one of the model's files or one of its Excel sheets changes, Julia notices at the next using and builds it again, once.\nRunning with other parameters or inputs works as always and needs no rebuild.\nA unit module of the model's own (@refunits) is registered with Unitful both when it's defined (Unitful.register(MyUnits) after the module) and when the package loads it (an __init__ that does the same), as PySD writes it for translated models.\n\nPySD (translate_to_julia(model; backend = \"dsl\")) writes Vensim models in this form, package files included.","title":"Models as packages"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Reference","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Writing-and-building-models","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Writing and building models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Inputs:-Excel,-lookups,-other-models","page":"Reference","text":"These are written inside @equations, where they are found without being imported.","title":"Inputs: Excel, lookups, other models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Arrays","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Running-and-results","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Running and results"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Precompiling-a-model","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Precompiling a model"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#The-equations-as-LaTeX","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"The equations as LaTeX"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Helpers-for-translated-ODE-and-MTK-models","page":"Reference","text":"Models translated with PySD's older backends (backend=\"ode\" or \"mtk\") are plain Julia ODE functions or ModelingToolkit systems that call these helpers.  Models written with @equations, or translated with backend=\"dsl\", don't need them.","title":"Helpers for translated ODE and MTK models"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Helpers-for-Vensim-functions","page":"Reference","text":"Function Vensim equivalent\npysd_xidz(x, y, z) XIDZ: x / y, or z when y == 0\npysd_zidz(x, y) ZIDZ: x / y, or 0 when y == 0\npysd_pulse(t, start, width) PULSE\npysd_pulse_train(t, start, interval, width, end) PULSE TRAIN\npysd_ramp(t, slope, start, end) RAMP\npysd_step(t, height, step_time) STEP\npysd_log_base(x, base) LOG\npysd_logical_and(a, b), pysd_logical_or(a, b), pysd_logical_not(a) :AND:, :OR:, :NOT:\npysd_random_0_1(), pysd_random_uniform(…), pysd_random_normal(…), pysd_random_exponential(…) the RANDOM functions (a new number at every evaluation)\npysd_vector_sort_order, pysd_vector_rank, pysd_vector_reorder the VECTOR functions\npysd_allocate_by_priority, pysd_allocate_available ALLOCATE BY PRIORITY, ALLOCATE AVAILABLE","title":"Helpers for Vensim functions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Excel-and-data-files","page":"Reference","text":"Excel files are cached in memory: one read per file per session.","title":"Excel and data files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Changing-a-model's-inputs-between-runs","page":"Reference","text":"A generated ODE model's params(; name = value, ...) builds its constants, lookups and data with the given values in place of its own (see PySD's documentation, \"Scenarios\").","title":"Changing a model's inputs between runs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Fixed-delays-and-samples","page":"Reference","text":"What the ODE and MTK backends write for DELAY FIXED and SAMPLE IF TRUE: records at the model's ticks, which a solver stops at (Solvers and the model's ticks).","title":"Fixed delays and samples"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Results-and-versions","page":"Reference","text":"","title":"Results and versions"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#PySD.@equations","page":"Reference","text":"@equations [name] begin\n    @parameters begin\n        τ = 10, [unit = u\"minute\", description = \"Characteristic time\"]\n    end\n    @variables begin\n        temperature(t), [unit = u\"°F\"]\n    end\n    ∂t(temperature) ~ -(temperature - room_temperature) / τ\n    temperature(t₀) ~ 180\nend\n\nA set of equations with the declarations of their names.  @parameters are given values, @variables have an equation (x(t) when they change with time); each declaration carries [unit = …] and optionally description, nonnegative, input, output.  Equations use ~.  With a name, the set is a component.  Model builds a model from one or more sets.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.@equations"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.EquationSet","page":"Reference","text":"EquationSet\n\nWhat @equations returns: declarations, equations and the Julia values they use.  A set with a name is a component; its names get the name as a prefix (retailer.inventory).  defined_in is the module the set is written in: a model built from it keeps its compiled code there (so that a package can precompile it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.EquationSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.Model","page":"Reference","text":"Model(sets…; time, time_unit, saveat = step(time), inputs = (;))\n\nBuild a model from equation sets (views, components, connections): join them, check the declarations, units, dimensions and equations, sort the equations and generate the code that runs it.  time = t₀:Δt:t_final; inputs are the input sheets, by name.  numerics = :python keeps Python PySD's last digits (for translated models: the C maths library, numpy's order of additions, LAPACK's gesv); the default, :julia, uses Julia's own functions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.Model"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.StatefulOperator","page":"Reference","text":"StatefulOperator\n\nWhat smooth, delay, trend, sample_if_true and the fixed delay x(t - τ) implement: how many hidden stocks they have, their initial values, their rates of change and the operator's value.  Model creates the hidden stocks.  A new operator is a new subtype with these methods:\n\nnstates(op)\ninitial_states(op, x, args...; init)\nrates!(d, op, s, x, args...)\noutput(op, s, x, args...)\n\n(s are the hidden stocks, x the input, args the other arguments).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.StatefulOperator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.check_units","page":"Reference","text":"check_units(model)\n\nThe equations of model whose units don't add up, as a vector of issues (file, line, variable, what differs); empty when they all do.  Same unit for +, -, min, max, comparisons and the branches of ifelse (units of one kind but another size, EJ and TJ, need a conversion factor); *, / and ^ combine units; exp, log and trigonometric functions take NoUnits; ∂t(x) has the unit of x per unit of time; the delay times of smooth, delay and x(t - τ) are times.  run_model(model; check_units = true) checks before running (:warn to run anyway).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.check_units"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.ExcelSheet","page":"Reference","text":"excel(path, sheet)\n\nAn Excel sheet, given to Model by name in inputs and read in @parameters with constant, lookup and data.  A different scenario is a different sheet given to the run: run_model(m; inputs = (; scen = excel(…))).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.ExcelSheet"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.constant","page":"Reference","text":"constant(sheet, cells; transpose = false)\n\nA number or an array from a cell, a range or a named range (Vensim's GET DIRECT CONSTANTS; transpose = true for its *).  The declaration gives it its dimensions: rows are the first, columns the second.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.constant"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.lookup","page":"Reference","text":"lookup(sheet, x, cells)\n\nA lookup read from Excel (Vensim's GET DIRECT LOOKUPS): x are the cells of the x values (a named range, or a row number or column letter), cells those of the y values; called as f(x).  With dimensions, one series per element: f(x) is an array, and with an array x the dimensions line up by name. It is read when its declaration gives it its dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.lookup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.data","page":"Reference","text":"data(sheet, time, cells; method = :interpolate)\n\nA data series read from Excel (Vensim's GET DIRECT DATA), called with time, d(t).  method is Vensim's keyword: :interpolate, :hold_backward, :look_forward or :raw.\n\n\n\n\n\ndata(parents, name)\n\nA data series from outside the model (Vensim's data variables without an equation): parents is an input given to Model and to the run, a tuple of sources, and name the series' name; the first source that has it gives it. A source is a run of another model (run_model(world)) or a file of series (series_file).  Called with time, d(t), it interpolates linearly between the source's times; with dimensions, they line up by name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.series_file","page":"Reference","text":"series_file(path)\n\nData series read from a .csv or .tab file, as Python PySD's data_files: the times in the first column and a column per series (or the times in the first row and a row per series), named as in Vensim, Temperature change or GDP[EU], Real demand[agriculture, EU] for the elements of an array.  A source of data: names and elements match the model's as the translation writes them (lower case, other characters as _).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.series_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.piecewise_linear","page":"Reference","text":"piecewise_linear(x₁ => y₁, x₂ => y₂, …)\n\nA lookup: called as f(x), it interpolates linearly between the points and keeps the first or last value outside them, as Vensim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.piecewise_linear"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.sum_over","page":"Reference","text":"sum_over(x, d)\nsum_over(x, (d1, d2, …))\n\nThe sum over dimension d, or over several at once, which the result no longer has (Vensim's SUM(x[d!]), SUM(x[d1!, d2!]); Julia's sum(x; dims = d) keeps d with length 1).  maximum_over, minimum_over and prod_over likewise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.sum_over"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.previous","page":"Reference","text":"previous(x, d)\n\nEach element takes the value of the one before it along dimension d; the first takes zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.previous"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.run_model","page":"Reference","text":"run_model(model; params = (;), inputs = (;), check_units = false)\n\nRun model from t₀ to tfinal with Euler steps of Δt, as Vensim, and save every saveat.  params changes parameters by name ((; τ = 5)), inputs input sheets (`(; scenw = excel(…))).checkunits = truechecks the units first ([checkunits](@ref)) and stops if they don't add up;:warn` prints the issues and runs anyway.\n\n\n\n\n\nrun_model(model, solver; params = (;), inputs = (;), check_units = false, kwargs...)\n\nRun model with a SciML ODE solver instead of Vensim's Euler steps, and save every saveat, as run_model(model).  kwargs go to solve (dt is Δt unless given, abstol, reltol, …).\n\nEuler() with steps of Δt gives Vensim's values.  Fixed delays and sample_if_true move only at the model's ticks, every Δt, whatever the solver: it stops at each, and its own steps in between integrate the stocks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.run_model"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.Results","page":"Reference","text":"Results\n\nWhat run_model returns: res.time, and the values of every name by name, res[:temperature] (a vector over the saved times; parameters and constants give their value).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.Results"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.ODEProblem-Tuple{Model}","page":"Reference","text":"ODEProblem(model; params = (;), inputs = (;), kwargs...)\n\nThe model as a SciML ODEProblem from t₀ to tfinal, with the given parameters and input sheets (as `runmodel's).  Solve it with any ODE solver (Euler()withdt = Δtis Vensim's);Results(model, sol)gives the values of every name from the solution.kwargsgo toODEProblem`.\n\nA model with fixed delays, sample_if_true or switches on the time (t ≥ 2015, a pulse) gets a callback that stops the solver at every step of Δt (the model's ticks), where they move or switch, as Vensim's.  With another solver than Euler() with dt = Δt, equations written for steps of Δt (that use Δt as a value, test the time for equality, or keep a stock non-negative) are named in a warning: those are the ones that may mean something else.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.ODEProblem"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#PySD.@precompile_model","page":"Reference","text":"@precompile_model name = expr\n\nIn a package: const name = expr, a Model, built and run once while Julia precompiles the package, so that the package saves the model with all that building and running it compiled.  Later sessions load it ready to run: using MyModel; run_model(MyModel.name).\n\nmodule MyModel\nusing PySD\n@precompile_model model = include(\"../mymodel.jl\")\nend\n\nJulia precompiles the package again when one of its files, or an Excel sheet the model reads, changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.@precompile_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_export_latex","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_export_latex(sys; filename=nothing)\n\nRender a ModelingToolkit ODESystem as LaTeX equations.\n\nIf filename is given the LaTeX string is written to that file (wrapped in a minimal document preamble so it can be compiled standalone).  The raw LaTeX string is always returned.\n\nExample\n\ninclude(\"my_model.jl\")          # defines `sys`\npysd_export_latex(sys)                         # returns LaTeX string\npysd_export_latex(sys; filename=\"eqs.tex\")     # also writes to file\n\n\n\n\n\npysd_export_latex(m::Model; filename = nothing)\n\nThe equations of a model written with @equations, as LaTeX, the way they are written: a section per set of equations (a view of a translated model), with its equations (∂t(x) as dx/dt, sum_over as Σ, ifelse as cases, selections as x[a]) and a table of its names, units and descriptions.  Needs Latexify.jl (using Latexify).  With filename, it also writes a document that compiles on its own.\n\nusing PySD, Latexify\nm = include(\"model_dsl/model.jl\")\npysd_export_latex(m; filename = \"model.tex\")\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_export_latex"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_vector_select","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_vector_select(sel, expr, missing_vals, numerical_action, error_action)\n\nVensim's VECTOR SELECT, as Python PySD: the elements of expr where sel is non-zero are combined by numerical_action (0 sum of selexpr, 1 product of selexpr, 2 min of selexpr, 3 max of selexpr, 4 mean of sel*expr, 5 product of expr^sel, 6 sum, 7 product, 8 min, 9 max, 10 mean of expr). missing_vals is returned when sel is all zeros.  error_action 1 (or 3) raises when sel is all zeros, 2 (or 3) when it has more than one non-zero. sel and expr are broadcast together (e.g. a matrix and a vector).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_vector_select"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_not_implemented","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_not_implemented(name, args...)\n\nWhat PySD's translator writes for a Vensim or Stella function it does not translate: an error naming it when the model evaluates it, as Python PySD's not_implemented_function (the translation warns that the model will crash).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_not_implemented"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_xlsx_read_constant","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_xlsx_read_constant(path, sheet, name; transpose=false)\n\nRead a scalar or array constant from an Excel named range or cell reference. The name may end with * to indicate transposition (Vensim convention).\n\n\n\n\n\npysd_xlsx_read_constant(path, sheet, names::Vector; transpose=false)\n\nRead multiple named ranges from Excel and concatenate them into a single array. Each element of names is either a String (range name to read from Excel) or a Vector{Float64} (literal values to insert directly).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_xlsx_read_constant"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_xlsx_read_series","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_xlsx_read_series(path, sheet, x_name, y_names::Vector{String})\n\nRead multiple y-series from Excel sharing the same x-axis. Returns (xs, ys_list) where ys_list is a Vector{Vector{Float64}}.\n\n\n\n\n\npysd_xlsx_read_series(path, sheet, x_row_or_col, y_cell)\n\nRead x/y series data from Excel for lookups or time-series data. Handles three Vensim reference modes:\n\nRow mode: x_row_or_col is a number (row), y_cell is a cell ref\nColumn mode: x_row_or_col is a column letter, y_cell is a cell ref\nName mode: both are named ranges\n\nReturns (xs::Vector{Float64}, ys::Vector{Float64}).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_xlsx_read_series"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_xlsx_build_lookup_dispatch","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_xlsx_build_lookup_dispatch(path, sheet, x_name, y_name; n=1, method=:interpolate)\n\nRead a (possibly 2D) lookup or data series from Excel and return a vector of interpolation functions, one per series of the y data.  With named ranges the series are the columns (or rows) of the y range; in row or column mode (x_name a row number or a column letter, y_name a cell) they are the n rows (or columns) from y_name.  method is a DATA keyword (:interpolate, :hold_backward, :look_forward), as in pysd_build_tab_itp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_xlsx_build_lookup_dispatch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_tab_read_series","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_tab_read_series(filepath, colname) -> (times, vals)\n\nRead a tab- or comma-separated Vensim output file and return the time series for the column named colname.  Matching is case-insensitive and leading/trailing whitespace is ignored.\n\nAccepts Unix, Windows and old Mac (CR only) line endings.  Uses only Julia Base functions — no extra packages required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_tab_read_series"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_build_tab_itp","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_build_tab_itp(vals, times, method)\n\nBuild a DataInterpolations interpolation object from (times, vals) using the Vensim interpolation method symbol:\n\n:interpolate            → LinearInterpolation\n:raw                    → the value at the data times, NaN in between\n:hold_backward           → ConstantInterpolation (hold previous value)\n:look_forward            → ConstantInterpolation(dir=:right) (next value)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_build_tab_itp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_input","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_input(given, name, default)\n\nThe value of the model input name: given[name] when it is there, converted to the kind of value the model has (see below), otherwise default.\n\nA constant takes a number, or an array of the constant's own size (a number fills the whole array).\nA lookup or data series takes anything pysd_lookup accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_input"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_lookup","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_lookup(value)\n\nA lookup or data series from value, called as the model calls it (f(x), or f(i, x), f(i, j, x) when it is subscripted):\n\na function, used as it is;\nan interpolation from DataInterpolations.jl (any kind), the same for every element;\n(x, y): two vectors, interpolated linearly and held constant outside x, as Vensim lookups; the same for every element;\n(x, Y): a vector and a matrix with one column per element of a one-dimensional subscripted lookup;\na number: the same value everywhere.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_lookup"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.PySDFunction","page":"Reference","text":"PySDFunction{N}(f)\n\nA lookup or data series of a model's parameters, called with N arguments (f(x), f(i, x), ...).  Every lookup with N arguments has this type, whatever function it holds, so replacing one (with params) does not change the type of the parameters and the model is not compiled again.\n\nCalls with integer indices and a Float64 go through a FunctionWrapper (FunctionWrappers.jl, as SciML does to avoid recompiling); other argument types (e.g. the numbers of an automatic-differentiation solver) call f directly.  The result is a number of the type of the last argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.PySDFunction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_check_inputs","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_check_inputs(given, values::NamedTuple)\n\nThrow an error naming the entries of given that are not among the model's constants, lookups and data (values), e.g. a misspelt name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_check_inputs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_check_fixed","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_check_fixed(given, fixed::NamedTuple)\n\nThrow an error if given has a new value for a constant whose value was used when the model was translated (fixed: name => what it fixed, e.g. the number of stages of a DELAY FIXED): that needs the model translated again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_check_fixed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_file","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_file(dir, file, files)\n\nThe path of the input file (as written in the model, relative to the model file's folder dir), or of its replacement in files (a Dict from the name in the model, or its base name, to another path; relative paths are taken from dir).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_file"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.PySDTicks","page":"Reference","text":"PySDTicks(t₀, Δt)\n\nThe records of a run of a translated model: one per fixed delay and sample, by a key (a number or a name), a value per tick.  While a solver runs, the tick is the callback's count (a step that ends at the next tick, before the callback has moved to it, is still in the tick before); afterwards it is the last tick at or before the time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.PySDTicks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#PySD.PySDRecord","page":"Reference","text":"PySDRecord(ticks, key, n, I...)\n\nWhich record a translated equation reads: of ticks, the one of key (a fixed delay, a sample, a RANDOM call's stream), n ticks back (a fixed delay's), element I of an array.  One argument instead of several, built with plain numbers when the equation is written: Symbolics registers a method for every combination of plain and symbolic arguments, 3ⁿ of them, so few arguments keep PySD quick to compile.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.PySDRecord"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_fixed_delay","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_fixed_delay(ticks, key, n, init, t, I...)\n\nDELAY FIXED: the input recorded n ticks before the tick at t, or, before the first n ticks, init as it was at the first tick (an initial value that is an expression, such as the input itself, is its value at the start); with indices I, that element of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_fixed_delay"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_sampled","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_sampled(ticks, key, condition, x, init, t, I...)\n\nSAMPLE IF TRUE.  At a tick being recorded: x if condition holds, else what it took at the tick before (init at the first).  Otherwise, what it took at the tick at t.  With indices I, an element: condition, x and init are that element's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_sampled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_tick_time","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_tick_time(ticks, t)\n\nThe time the model sees.  While a solver runs, a step that ends at the next tick, before the callback has moved to it (Runge-Kutta's last stage), is still in the tick before: it sees the time just before the next tick, so a switch at a tick (STEP, PULSE, Time >= 2015) changes the rates from that tick on.  Otherwise, t.  (Back by more than SMALL_VENSIM, which PULSE TRAIN allows for.)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_tick_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_tick_uniform","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_tick_uniform(record, t)\npysd_tick_normal(record, t)\npysd_tick_exponential(record, t)\n\nA standard draw (uniform on [0, 1), normal (0, 1), exponential (1)) for the tick at t, the same for the whole tick: from a generator seeded from the record's key (the RANDOM call's stream), its element and the tick.  The same draws in every run, with any solver.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_tick_uniform"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_record!","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_record!(ticks, key, x)\n\nRecords x for key at the current tick: a fixed delay's input, or a sample's value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_record!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_ticks_callback","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_ticks_callback(ticks, record!; t_final)\n\nA SciML callback (with SciMLBase loaded) that makes the solver stop at every tick of ticks up to t_final and calls record!(u, t) there, to record the fixed delays and samples from the state.  pysd_free_ticks!(ticks) after the solve lets the records be read by time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_ticks_callback"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_begin_tick!","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_begin_tick!(ticks, t)\npysd_end_tick!(ticks)\n\nThe next tick, at the time t: between the two, the model is computed from the state there and its fixed delays and samples recorded (pysd_record!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_begin_tick!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_reset_ticks!","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_reset_ticks!(ticks)\n\nForget every record and count the ticks from the start: at the start of a run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_reset_ticks!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.pysd_free_ticks!","page":"Reference","text":"pysd_free_ticks!(ticks)\n\nAfter a solve: the tick is the time's again, to read the records at any time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.pysd_free_ticks!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.save_results","page":"Reference","text":"save_results(sol, state_map, dim_labels, path)\n\nODE-backend variant.  state_map is a vector of (name, index, dim_names) and dim_labels maps each subscript range name to its element labels. Scalar state variables are saved with dimension (time,).  Subscripted state variables get one NetCDF dimension per subscript range, named after the range and holding its element labels, in the file order (time, dim_1, ..., dim_n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.save_results"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#PySD.check_compat","page":"Reference","text":"check_compat(built_with::VersionNumber)\n\nVerify that the installed PySD.jl is compatible with the version the model was translated against.  Raises an error when the major version differs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"PySD.check_compat"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Running-a-model","page":"Running a model","text":"run_model runs a model with Euler steps of Δt, as Vensim, and saves every saveat:\n\nr = run_model(m)\nr[:x]\n81-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 1.025\n 1.050625\n 1.0768906249999999\n 1.103812890625\n 1.131408212890625\n 1.1596934182128906\n 1.188685753668213\n 1.2184028975099184\n 1.2488629699476663\n ⋮\n 5.917228062214787\n 6.065158763770157\n 6.216787732864411\n 6.372207426186021\n 6.531512611840672\n 6.6948004271366885\n 6.862170437815106\n 7.033724698760484\n 7.209567816229495\n\nThe results give each name's values at the saved times (r.time): a vector of numbers, or of arrays with their dimensions.  A parameter gives its value.","title":"Running a model"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Running-again-with-other-values","page":"Running a model","text":"Model compiles the model once.  After that, running it with other parameter values or other input sheets is fast, and compiles nothing:\n\nr2 = run_model(m; params = (; k = 0.2))\nr3 = run_model(m; inputs = (; scenario = excel(\"scenarios.xlsx\", \"High\")))\n\nThe one exception is a parameter that sets the structure of an operator, such as the time of a fixed delay (the number of steps it keeps) or the order of a smooth.  Changing it needs the model built again, and run_model says so with an error rather than ignoring the new value.","title":"Running again with other values"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Checking-units","page":"Running a model","text":"run_model(m; check_units = true)      # stops if an equation's units don't add up\nrun_model(m; check_units = :warn)     # prints them and runs\ncheck_units(m)                        # the list","title":"Checking units"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Results-as-tables","page":"Running a model","text":"The results are a Tables.jl table, with a column for the time and one per name and element, named as in Vensim's outputs and Python PySD's DataFrame (stock[north,a]); and DimStack(r) is a DimensionalData DimStack, a layer per name over the time (Ti) and its dimensions, as xarray's datasets:\n\nusing DataFrames, CSV\ndf = DataFrame(r)                       # a column per name and element\nCSV.write(\"results.csv\", r)\nst = DimStack(r)                        # st[:x][Ti = At(10.0)]\n\nParameters are in both, as in Python PySD's DataFrame (a column at every time in the table; without the time in the DimStack); lookups and data series are functions, in neither.","title":"Results as tables"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#Data-from-other-models-or-from-files","page":"Running a model","text":"A model can take series from outside it, as Vensim's data variables without an equation: from another model's run (a regional model fed by a global one), or from a file of series:\n\n@parameters begin\n    temperature_change = data(parents, :temperature_change), [unit = u\"K\"]\nend\n…\ndamage ~ f(temperature_change(t))\n\nparents is an input, a tuple of sources given when the model is built and when it runs; each series comes from the first source that has it, by name, interpolated linearly between the source's times.  A source is a run (run_model(…)) or a file, series_file(\"history.tab\"): a .csv or .tab with the times in the first column and a column per series named as in Vensim (Temperature change, GDP[EU]), or a row per series, as Python PySD's data_files.\n\nw  = run_model(world)\neu = run_model(europe; inputs = (; parents = (w,)))\nw2 = run_model(world; params = (; k = 0.2))           # another world …\neu2 = run_model(europe; inputs = (; parents = (w2,)))  # … the same Europe, no rebuild\neu3 = run_model(europe; inputs = (; parents = (series_file(\"world.tab\"),)))   # from a file","title":"Data from other models or from files"},{"category":"section","location":"running/#The-equations-as-LaTeX","page":"Running a model","text":"using Latexify                          # loads PySD.jl's LaTeX export\npysd_export_latex(m; filename = \"model.tex\")\n\nwrites the equations as they are written: a section per set of equations (a view of a translated model), stocks as dx/dt, sum_over as Σ, ifelse as cases, selections as x[a], and a table of the names with their units and descriptions.  Without filename it returns the LaTeX as a string.","title":"The equations as LaTeX"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Writing-models","page":"Writing models","text":"A model is one or more sets of equations, written with @equations, built into a Model.\n\nusing PySD, Unitful\n\nteacup = @equations begin\n    @parameters begin\n        room_temperature = 70, [unit = u\"°F\", description = \"Room temperature\"]\n        characteristic_time = 10, [unit = u\"minute\"]\n    end\n    @variables begin\n        teacup_temperature(t), [unit = u\"°F\"]\n        heat_loss_to_room(t), [unit = u\"°F/minute\"]\n    end\n\n    ∂t(teacup_temperature) ~ -heat_loss_to_room          # Newton's law of cooling\n    teacup_temperature(t₀) ~ 180\n    heat_loss_to_room ~ (teacup_temperature - room_temperature) / characteristic_time\nend\n\nm = Model(teacup; time = 0:0.125:30, time_unit = u\"minute\")\nModel(4 names, 1 stocks, 1 states)","title":"Writing models"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Declarations","page":"Writing models","text":"Every name is declared before it is used, with its unit:\n\n@parameters: what is known before the run, with its value (k = 0.1, [unit = u\"1/yr\"]).  A run can give it another (run_model(m; params = (; k = 0.2))).\n@variables: what an equation computes; x(t) when it changes with time, x alone when it is computed once, at the start.\n\nAfter the unit, optionally:\n\ndescription = \"…\", kept with the results and in the LaTeX export;\nnonnegative = true, for a stock that can't go below zero: its outflow is cut to what empties it in one step of Δt;\ninput = true, output = true, for the variables a component takes from and gives to the rest of the model (Components).\n\nNames without a unit are NoUnits.  Units are Unitful.jl's (u\"kg/yr\"), and a model can declare its own (@unit case \"case\" Case 1 false in a module, registered with Unitful.register).","title":"Declarations"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Equations","page":"Writing models","text":"Equations use ~, and any Julia expression on the right:\n\nEquation Meaning\ng ~ k * x g at every step\n∂t(x) ~ inflow - outflow a stock: its rate of change\nx(t₀) ~ 1 the stock's initial value\ny ~ x(t₀) the value x had at the start (Vensim's INITIAL)\ny ~ x(t - 2; init = 0) the value x had 2 time units ago (DELAY FIXED)\n\nt is the time, t₀ the initial time and Δt the time step.  ifelse(c, a, b) is Vensim's IF THEN ELSE, and the usual Julia functions (min, max, exp, log, …) are Vensim's.  Vensim functions lists them all.\n\nThe order of the equations doesn't matter: Model sorts them, and stops with an error that names the loop (a → b → a) when some depend on each other with no stock in between.","title":"Equations"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Operators-with-memory","page":"Writing models","text":"Function Vensim\nsmooth(x, τ), smooth(x, τ; init = x0) SMOOTH, SMOOTHI\nsmooth(x, τ; order = 3) (; order = n, init = x0) SMOOTH3, SMOOTH N\ndelay(x, τ), delay(x, τ; init = x0) DELAY1, DELAY1I\ndelay(x, τ; order = 3) (; order = n, init = x0) DELAY3, DELAY N\ntrend(x, τ; init = r0) TREND\nx(t - τ; init = x0) DELAY FIXED\nsample_if_true(c, x; init = x0) SAMPLE IF TRUE\nrandom_uniform(lo, hi; seed), random_normal(lo, hi, mean, sd; seed), … RANDOM UNIFORM, RANDOM NORMAL, … (one draw per step)\nx(t₀) INITIAL\n\nThe smooths, delays and trends have hidden stocks of their own, which the model integrates with the rest.  The fixed delay and the sample move by whole steps of Δt, as Vensim (Solvers and the model's ticks).  New operators are new subtypes of StatefulOperator.","title":"Operators with memory"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Lookups-and-data","page":"Writing models","text":"@parameters begin\n    effect = piecewise_linear(0 => 1.0, 1 => 0.8, 2 => 0.5), [unit = NoUnits]\n    price_history = data(sheet, \"year\", \"price\"), [unit = u\"USD\"]\nend\n…\ndemand ~ base_demand * effect(price / reference_price)\nprice ~ price_history(t)\n\nA lookup is called with its argument; it interpolates linearly between its points and keeps the first or last value outside them.  A data series is a lookup of the time.","title":"Lookups and data"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Excel","page":"Writing models","text":"sheet = excel(\"inputs.xlsx\", \"Sheet1\")\n@parameters begin\n    capacity[region] = constant(sheet, \"B2:B5\"), [unit = u\"MW\"]\n    efficiency = lookup(sheet, \"A10:A20\", \"B10:B20\"), [unit = NoUnits]\n    demand = data(sheet, \"year\", \"demand\"; method = :interpolate), [unit = u\"MW\"]\nend\n\nconstant, lookup and data read cells or named ranges, as Vensim's GET XLS and GET DIRECT functions.  A sheet given to Model by name (Model(…; inputs = (; scenario = sheet))) can be replaced by another for a run (run_model(m; inputs = (; scenario = excel(…)))).","title":"Excel"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Arrays","page":"Writing models","text":"Dimensions are DimensionalData.jl dimensions, Vensim's subscript ranges:\n\nusing PySD, DimensionalData\n\nconst region = Dim{:region}([:north, :south])\n\nregions = @equations begin\n    @parameters begin\n        initial_population[region] = [10.0, 20.0], [unit = NoUnits]\n        growth = 0.02, [unit = u\"1/yr\"]\n    end\n    @variables begin\n        population(t)[region], [unit = NoUnits]\n        births(t)[region], [unit = u\"1/yr\"]\n        total(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n    end\n    ∂t(population) ~ births\n    population(t₀) ~ initial_population\n    births[region = At(:north)] ~ growth * population[region = At(:north)]\n    births[region = Not(At(:north))] ~ 2 * growth .* population[region = Not(At(:north))]\n    total ~ sum_over(population, region)\nend\n\nr = run_model(Model(regions; time = 0:1:10, time_unit = u\"yr\"))\nr[:population][end]\n\u001b[90m┌ \u001b[39m\u001b[38;5;209m2-element \u001b[39mDimArray{Float64, 1}\u001b[90m ┐\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m├────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────── dims ┐\u001b[39m\n  \u001b[38;5;209m↓ \u001b[39m\u001b[38;5;209mregion\u001b[39m Categorical{Symbol} \u001b[38;5;209m[:north, :south]\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;244mForwardOrdered\u001b[39m\n\u001b[90m└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\u001b[39m\n \u001b[38;5;209m:north\u001b[39m  12.1899\n \u001b[38;5;209m:south\u001b[39m  29.6049\n\nx[region] declares an array over region; its equation is for all of it, with Julia's broadcasting (.*).\nAn equation can be for a part: x[region = At(:south)] ~ …, and Not(At(…)) for the rest.  The parts can't overlap: Model says which equations do.\nsum_over(x, d), prod_over, maximum_over, minimum_over reduce over a dimension (Vensim's SUM(x[d!])); previous(x, d) shifts along one.\nMatrices are matrices: inv(I - A), A * v.","title":"Arrays"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Components","page":"Writing models","text":"A set with a name is a component.  Several of them, with an unnamed set that connects them, make a model; each component's names are kept apart (retailer.inventory, wholesaler.inventory):\n\nfunction stage(; name, target = 12)\n    @equations name begin\n        @parameters begin\n            target_inventory = target, [unit = u\"case\"]\n        end\n        @variables begin\n            incoming_orders(t) = 4, [unit = u\"case/wk\", input = true]\n            shipments(t), [unit = u\"case/wk\", output = true]\n            inventory(t), [unit = u\"case\", nonnegative = true]\n            …\n        end\n        …\n    end\nend\n\nretailer, wholesaler = stage(; name = :retailer), stage(; name = :wholesaler)\nconnections = @equations begin\n    retailer.incoming_orders ~ customer_orders\n    wholesaler.incoming_orders ~ retailer.orders_placed\n    …\nend\nm = Model(retailer, wholesaler, connections; time = 0:1:52, time_unit = u\"wk\")\n\nAn input variable's own equation (= 4) is the value it takes when the model doesn't connect it.  The equations are Julia code: a component is a function, and a choice in it (an ordering rule) can be a type, chosen by dispatch.","title":"Components"},{"category":"section","location":"writing/#Building","page":"Writing models","text":"Model(sets…; time = t₀:Δt:t_final, time_unit, saveat = step(time), inputs = (;),\n      numerics = :julia)\n\nchecks the declarations and the dimensions, and sorts and compiles the equations.  saveat is Vensim's SAVEPER.  check_units lists the equations whose units don't add up, and run_model(m; check_units = true) checks them before running (:warn to only print them).","title":"Building"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Running-the-tests","page":"Development","text":"The tests use the reference models of SDXorg/test-models, included as a git submodule:\n\ngit submodule update --init test/test-models\njulia --project -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nSet PYSD_TEST_MODELS to use another checkout of test-models (e.g. tests/test-models/tests in a PySD checkout).  Without test-models the reference tests are skipped with a warning.","title":"Running the tests"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Building-this-documentation","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nwrites the site to docs/build (open docs/build/index.html).  The code examples run while it builds, so a change that breaks one fails the build. On GitHub, every push to main publishes it as the development version, and every release tag as that version and stable.","title":"Building this documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Version-compatibility","page":"Development","text":"Generated models embed the PySD.jl version they were translated against. On load, check_compat verifies that the installed PySD.jl is compatible. A major-version mismatch raises an error; a minor-version mismatch emits a warning.","title":"Version compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Contributing","page":"Development","text":"See CONTRIBUTING.md.","title":"Contributing"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#PySD.jl-news","page":"News","text":"What changes in each version, newest first.  Each version's release has the full notes and the tests run before it.","title":"PySD.jl news"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Unreleased","page":"News","text":"","title":"Unreleased"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Added","page":"News","text":"RANDOM in models written with @equations: random_0_1, random_uniform, random_normal and random_exponential, drawn once per tick and held in between, from a generator seeded from the seed, the call, the element and the tick: every run draws the same, with any solver. dims = d draws per element of d where the arguments are numbers.\npysd_not_implemented(name, args...): what PySD's translator writes for a function it does not translate, as Python PySD: the model stops with an error naming it when it evaluates it (before, a placeholder 0.0 or an undefined name).","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed","page":"News","text":"RANDOM NORMAL and RANDOM EXPONENTIAL as Python PySD's in translated models: pysd_tick_normal(record, a, b, t) and pysd_tick_exponential(record, b, t) draw truncated (again while outside), where 0.5.0's translations cut the draws at the bounds, and took RANDOM EXPONENTIAL's third argument as its scale (it is the shift; the fourth, the stretch).","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.5.0-(2026-11-01)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.5.0 (2026-11-01)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Added-2","page":"News","text":"RANDOM functions drawn once per tick, in translated models: pysd_tick_uniform, pysd_tick_normal and pysd_tick_exponential give a standard draw for the tick, from a generator seeded from the call's stream, the element and the tick.  So a draw holds between ticks, the values read after a run are the ones the run used, and every run, with any solver, draws the same.  (pysd_random_* draw at every call, and ignore the seed.)\nSwitches on the time exact at the ticks, in translated models (pysd_tick_time), as in models written with @equations since 0.4.0.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Changed","page":"News","text":"The warning for equations written for Euler steps of Δt names fewer: not an equation that depends on the time alone (parameters, data series, lookups, and fixed delays or samples of them), such as a finite difference of data, (data(t + Δt) - data(t)) / Δt, the same with any solver.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-2","page":"News","text":"PySD compiles in 40 s again (about 7 min with 0.4.0).  The functions 0.4.0 registered with Symbolics for MTK models took up to nine arguments, and Symbolics adds a method for every combination of plain and symbolic arguments: 19,437 for pysd_sampled.  The record they read is now one argument, PySDRecord(ticks, key, n, I...) (163 methods).  The calls of 0.4.0 still work in ODE models.\nRuns on several threads at once (a sweep, an ensemble): the input sheets the runs read are cached behind a lock.  Tested with two threads.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.4.0-(2026-11-01)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.4.0 (2026-11-01)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Added-3","page":"News","text":"Fixed delays and samples of models translated with PySD's ODE and MTK backends, with any solver (src/ticks.jl).  PySDTicks keeps a record per fixed delay and sample, a value per tick of the model; the generated code reads them (pysd_fixed_delay, pysd_sampled, registered with Symbolics for MTK) and pysd_ticks_callback makes a solver stop at every tick to record them.  Before, the translator wrote them as stocks that are exact only with Euler steps of TIME STEP.","title":"Added"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Changed-2","page":"News","text":"Fixed delays and SAMPLE IF TRUE keep histories instead of hidden stocks (#33).  With Euler steps of Δt (run_model(m), or Euler() with dt = Δt) they give Vensim's values as before, now copied exactly (the stocks gave s + (x - s), off in the last digit at times), and the state vector no longer holds a stage per step of delay.\nFixed delays and SAMPLE IF TRUE run with any solver.  They move at the model's ticks, every Δt, as Vensim: a solver now stops at each tick, where they record and move, and holds them between ticks.  Before, other solvers gave values that weren't Vensim's model (the delays acted as smooths of time Δt, and sample_if_true(t == year, x) never sampled).  Euler() with dt = Δt still gives Vensim's values; other solvers give more accurate stocks, never faster runs.\nSwitches on the time are exact at the ticks with any solver.  A model with t ≥ 2015, STEP, PULSE or PULSE TRAIN stops at the ticks too, and a solver's step ending at a tick sees the time just before it (Runge-Kutta's last stage saw the switch already on, a sixth of a step early with RK4).\nA warning names the equations written for Euler steps of Δt (Δt as a value, the time tested for equality, non-negative stocks) when a model is solved with any other solver than Euler() with dt = Δt.\nFirst runs compile faster (#34): each equation reads the state itself, so the function that calls them is much quicker to compile.  On a large model (about 71,000 states), the initial values, a step and a saved row compile in 113, 38 and 36 s, from 143, 52 and 55 s.\nDocumentation (#38), built with Documenter.jl and published at https://rogersamso.github.io/PySD.jl/: the guide, every Vensim function, the reference.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.3.2-(2026-10-29)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.3.2 (2026-10-29)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-3","page":"News","text":"Stiff solvers that compute the Jacobian with ForwardDiff (Rodas5P() and the other Rosenbrock methods, by default) work on models with arrays: the step writes into buffers of its state's number type when it isn't the run's (#31).","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.3.1-(2026-10-29)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.3.1 (2026-10-29)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#New","page":"News","text":"Derivatives and uncertainties through a run (#26).  A run computes in the number type of its parameters: with ForwardDiff.jl, the exact derivative of any result in a parameter (for calibration, or sensitivity), and with Measurements.jl, a parameter's uncertainty carried to every result, in one run.\nSciML's ODE solvers (#27). run_model(m, solver) runs a model with any of them and gives the same Results; ODEProblem(m) is the model as a SciML problem (callbacks, ensembles of runs, …), and Results(m, sol) the values from its solution. Euler with steps of Δt gives Vensim's values; with other solvers, fixed delays and sample_if_true aren't Vensim's, and run_model warns. Loaded with SciMLBase, which any SciML solver package loads.\nResults as tables (#28). Results is a Tables.jl table, a column per name and element named as in Vensim's outputs and Python PySD's DataFrame (stock[north,a]), so DataFrame(r) and CSV.write(path, r) work; DimStack(r) is a DimensionalData dataset, a layer per name over the time and its dimensions.","title":"New"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Project","page":"News","text":"MIT license (#23), a contributing guide (#22), and test coverage on Codecov (#24).","title":"Project"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Compatibility","page":"News","text":"Nothing changes for models translated for 0.3.0 or 0.2.x.  New dependency: Tables.jl (already loaded through DimensionalData and XLSX).  New optional one: SciMLBase (2.116 or later).","title":"Compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.3.0-(2026-10-28)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.3.0 (2026-10-28)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#New-2","page":"News","text":"The DSL (#21): models written in Julia with @equations, Model and run_model, every name declared with its unit and description; arrays with DimensionalData dimensions, smooths, delays, trends, sample-if-true, lookups, and inputs from Excel.\nUnits checked with check_units(m); other parameter values and input sheets without compiling again; data from other models' runs or files of series; models as packages (@precompile_model), loaded in seconds; Python's digits (numerics = :python); the equations as LaTeX.\nbenchmark/: a translated model timed against Python PySD, and their results compared.\n\nPySD's DSL backend (translate_to_julia(model, backend = \"dsl\")) writes Vensim and XMILE models in this language.","title":"New"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Compatibility-2","page":"News","text":"Models of the ODE and MTK backends translated for 0.2.x still load.  On Julia 1.10 a model's step sometimes allocates a few hundred bytes; from Julia 1.11 it allocates nothing.","title":"Compatibility"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.7-(2026-10-24)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.7 (2026-10-24)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Changed-3","page":"News","text":"The same digits as numpy: pysd_sum adds in numpy's pairwise order and pysd_sum_seq one element after the other, INVERT MATRIX uses LAPACK's gesv, and pysd_exp, pysd_ln, pysd_sin, … and pysd_power call the platform's C maths library, as numpy does.  SafeArray reads elements outside its size as NaN, as Python PySD.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.6-(2026-10-23)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.6 (2026-10-23)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-4","page":"News","text":"LN, SQRT, ARCSIN, ARCCOS and LOG out of their domain give NaN, as numpy, instead of a DomainError (a branch of IF THEN ELSE that isn't taken stopped the run) (#15).\nA named range local to several sheets is read from the requested sheet.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.5-(2026-10-23)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.5 (2026-10-23)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#New-3","page":"News","text":"Scenarios for generated ODE models: params(; name = value, …) builds the constants, lookups and data with other values, and what is computed from them follows; replacing a lookup doesn't compile the model again (#14).","title":"New"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-5","page":"News","text":"A series of one point has the same value everywhere, as in Python PySD.\nXLSX.jl 0.11 at least: with 0.10, named ranges failed.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.4-(2026-10-22)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.4 (2026-10-22)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-6","page":"News","text":"pysd_xidz and pysd_zidz treat denominators below 1e-6 as zero, also in MTK models (#12).\nExcel numbers stored as text are read as numbers, and square blocks of Excel series take their direction from the x range (#13).","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.3-(2026-10-22)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.3 (2026-10-22)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Changed-4","page":"News","text":"pysd_vector_select takes Vensim's five arguments and its 11 numerical actions; :raw data give values at the data times only; Excel series with missing values are filled as Python PySD does (#9).","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.2-(2026-10-22)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.2 (2026-10-22)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#New-4","page":"News","text":".xls files and delimited text files (.csv, .tab, …) for GET XLS and GET DIRECT CONSTANTS, LOOKUPS and DATA, as Python PySD (#7).","title":"New"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-7","page":"News","text":"A defined name takes precedence over a cell reference that looks the same.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.1-(2026-10-21)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.1 (2026-10-21)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Fixed-8","page":"News","text":"pysd_power with a negative base, pysd_pulse and pysd_pulse_train with Vensim's time tolerance, XMILE's PULSE, 0-based pysd_vector_sort_order, and .tab files with any line ending, as Vensim and Python PySD.","title":"Fixed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.2.0-(2026-10-21)","page":"News","text":"","title":"0.2.0 (2026-10-21)"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#Changed-5","page":"News","text":"save_results writes one NetCDF dimension per subscript range, as Python PySD's NetCDF output.","title":"Changed"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#New-5","page":"News","text":"ALLOCATE AVAILABLE and ALLOCATE BY PRIORITY; data from .tab and .csv files; a test suite against SDXorg/test-models, and CI.","title":"New"},{"category":"section","location":"news/#0.1.0-(2026-06-28)","page":"News","text":"The first version: the runtime functions for models generated by PySD's Julia builder.","title":"0.1.0 (2026-06-28)"},{"category":"section","location":"#PySD.jl","page":"Home","text":"System dynamics models in Julia: Vensim models translated by PySD, or models written directly in Julia.\n\nWrite a model as equations, with units and named dimensions (Writing models), or translate one from Vensim (Translating a Vensim model).\nRun it with Vensim's Euler steps, with the same results as Vensim and Python PySD, or with any of SciML's ODE solvers (Solvers and the model's ticks).\nRun it again with other parameters or input sheets without compiling anything: the many runs of a sensitivity analysis, derivatives with ForwardDiff.jl, uncertainties with Measurements.jl (Sensitivity and derivatives).\nLoad a large model in seconds by keeping it in a package (Models as packages).","title":"PySD.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"PySD.jl isn't in Julia's General registry yet.  Install it from GitHub:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(url = \"https://github.com/rogersamso/PySD.jl\")\n\nPkg.develop clones the repository (into ~/.julia/dev/PySD, on main; git checkout v0.3.2 there for a release).  Pkg.add(url = …) doesn't work: it can't clone the test models' git submodule.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#A-first-model","page":"Home","text":"A stock x that grows at a rate k:\n\nusing PySD, Unitful\n\ngrowth = @equations begin\n    @parameters begin\n        k = 0.1, [unit = u\"1/yr\", description = \"growth rate\"]\n    end\n    @variables begin\n        x(t), [unit = u\"kg\"]\n        g(t), [unit = u\"kg/yr\"]\n    end\n    g ~ k * x\n    ∂t(x) ~ g              # a stock: its rate of change\n    x(t₀) ~ 1              # and its initial value\nend\n\nm = Model(growth; time = 0:0.25:20, time_unit = u\"yr\")\nr = run_model(m)\nr[:x][end]                 # x at the final time\n7.209567816229495\n\nModel checks the equations and their units, sorts them and compiles the model once.  run_model takes Euler steps of Δt (here 0.25 years), as Vensim, and saves every year.  Running it again with another growth rate compiles nothing:\n\nrun_model(m; params = (; k = 0.2))[:x][end]\n49.56144106684244","title":"A first model"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-it-comes-from","page":"Home","text":"Writing models in Julia borrows from:\n\nModelingToolkit.jl: @equations takes the form of its @mtkmodel: @parameters and @variables blocks, ~ for equations, x(t) for what changes with time, and a unit and description after each declaration.\nBPTK-Py: stocks that can't go below zero (nonnegative = true), parts of a model written once and used several times, with their names in the results after the part's (retailer.inventory, wholesaler.inventory), and errors that name the algebraic loop (a → b → a).\nDimensionalData.jl: arrays with named dimensions, as xarray's in Python PySD.\nUnitful.jl: units, and checking that each equation's units add up.\nVensim and PySD: the functions and their behaviour, and the reference outputs the tests compare against (SDXorg/test-models).\n\nAlso related: StockFlow.jl, stock-and-flow diagrams in Julia, with composition and stratification.\n\nPySD.jl is released under the MIT License.","title":"Where it comes from"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#Translating-a-Vensim-model","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"A Vensim model (.mdl) becomes a PySD.jl model with PySD's Julia builder. It parses the model as Python PySD does, and writes the equations in PySD.jl's language (Writing models): readable Julia files that can be run, changed, and kept in version control.","title":"Translating a Vensim model"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#The-translator","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"The builder is not in a PySD release yet.  It is on the exp/julia-dsl branch of rogersamso/pysd:\n\ngit clone -b exp/julia-dsl https://github.com/rogersamso/pysd.git\npip install -e pysd\n\nimport pysd\npysd.translate_to_julia(\"my_model.mdl\", backend=\"dsl\", split_views=True)\n\nbackend=\"dsl\" writes PySD.jl's language.  (The other backends, \"ode\" and \"mtk\", write an older form: a plain ODE function or a ModelingToolkit system, which call the helpers listed under Helpers for translated ODE and MTK models.)\nsplit_views=True writes one file per view of the Vensim sketch, as the modeller grouped the equations.  Without it, one file holds them all.","title":"The translator"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#What-it-writes","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"A folder my_model_dsl/ next to the model:\n\nmy_model_dsl/\n├── my_model.jl        # includes the rest and ends with Model(…)\n├── units.jl           # the model's own units (@refunit), registered with Unitful\n├── dims.jl            # the subscript ranges, as DimensionalData dimensions\n├── inputs.jl          # the Excel files and sheets the model reads, by name\n├── <view>.jl          # one @equations set per view\n├── names.tsv          # Vensim's name of each variable, and its Julia name\n├── Project.toml       # the folder is also a package …\n└── src/MyModel.jl     # … that loads in seconds (Models as packages)\n\nNames become Julia names: lower case, _ for spaces (Teacup temperature → teacup_temperature).  The results are saved with Vensim's names (names.tsv), so they compare directly with Vensim's or Python PySD's outputs.\n\nThe last file, my_model.jl, builds the model:\n\nmy_model = Model(view_a, view_b, …, inputs, time = 1995:0.03125:2050, saveat = 1,\n                 time_unit = u\"yr\", numerics = :python)\n\nwith Vensim's INITIAL TIME, TIME STEP, FINAL TIME and SAVEPER. numerics = :python keeps Python PySD's last digits (the C maths library, numpy's order of additions), to compare with its results bit for bit; the default, :julia, uses Julia's own functions.","title":"What it writes"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#Running-it","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"A translated model needs the PySD.jl its translation is written for: the copy in PySD's checkout, as a git submodule (the model's package requires that version, and check_compat warns on load if another is installed):\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.develop(path = \"pysd/pysd/builders/julia/PySD.jl\")\n\nThen:\n\nusing PySD\nm = include(\"my_model_dsl/my_model.jl\")\nr = run_model(m)\n\nor, to build and compile it only once, as a package (Models as packages):\n\nusing Pkg; Pkg.develop(path = \"my_model_dsl\")\nusing MyModel\nr = run_model(MyModel.model)","title":"Running it"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#Input-sheets-and-scenarios","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"Constants, lookups and data that Vensim reads from Excel (GET DIRECT CONSTANTS, GET DIRECT LOOKUPS, GET DIRECT DATA and the GET XLS forms) are read from the sheets named in inputs.jl.  A scenario that is another sheet of the same layout is given to the run, with no rebuild:\n\nr = run_model(m; inputs = (; scenario = excel(\"scenarios.xlsx\", \"High\")))","title":"Input sheets and scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#Models-fed-by-other-models","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"Vensim data variables without an equation (to be filled from another model's results) become data(parents, :name): the series is taken from the runs or files given as parents (Data from other models or from files).\n\nw  = run_model(world)\neu = run_model(europe; inputs = (; parents = (w,)))","title":"Models fed by other models"},{"category":"section","location":"translating/#What-isn't-translated-yet","page":"Translating a Vensim model","text":"The translator stops with an error naming the equation for:\n\nFORECAST;\nthe RANDOM functions;\nVECTOR SELECT, VECTOR RANK, VECTOR SORT ORDER, VECTOR REORDER;\nALLOCATE BY PRIORITY, ALLOCATE AVAILABLE;\nGET TIME VALUE;\nACTIVE INITIAL inside an expression (on its own, it is translated).\n\nVensim functions lists every function with its form in PySD.jl.","title":"What isn't translated yet"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Solvers-and-the-model's-ticks","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"","title":"Solvers and the model's ticks"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Euler-steps-of-Δt,-as-Vensim","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"run_model(m) integrates the stocks as Vensim does by default: Euler steps of Δt (Vensim's TIME STEP).  At each step it computes every equation from the stocks, then moves each stock by its rate times Δt.  This gives Vensim's and Python PySD's values, which the tests check against SDXorg/test-models.\n\nThree times are involved:\n\nΔt (TIME STEP): the model's step.  The fixed delays and samples count it, and some equations are written for it (below).\ndt: the step a solver takes.  For run_model, dt = Δt.\nsaveat (SAVEPER): when results are saved.  Usually a multiple of Δt: a model may need a small Δt for accuracy, but its results are read yearly.","title":"Euler steps of Δt, as Vensim"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Other-solvers:-SciML","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"With a SciML solver package loaded, a model also runs with any of SciML's ODE solvers, and the results are the same Results:\n\nusing OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK                 # or OrdinaryDiffEq, for all of them\nr = run_model(m, Euler())                      # Vensim's steps, the same values as run_model(m)\nr = run_model(m, Euler(); dt = m.dt / 4)       # smaller steps: is Δt small enough?\nr = run_model(m, RK4())                        # a higher order\nr = run_model(m, BS3(); reltol = 1e-8)         # adaptive steps\n\nStiff models, with time constants far apart, take a stiff solver, such as Rodas5P() from OrdinaryDiffEqRosenbrock.\n\nODEProblem(m; params, inputs) is the model as a SciML problem, for what run_model doesn't do (callbacks, ensembles of runs, …), and Results(m, sol) the values of every name from its solution.\n\nWhat another solver is good for:\n\nChecking that Δt is small enough.  Euler's error shrinks with the step.  If the results at Δt/2 and Δt/4 differ from those at Δt, the model's stocks carry that much integration error; a higher-order or adaptive solver shows where they converge.\nMore accurate stocks for the same work, with a higher-order method.\nStiff models, which Euler only runs with a very small Δt.\n\nIt doesn't make a run faster when the model has fixed delays, samples or switches on the time: the solver stops at every tick (below), so it never takes steps longer than Δt.  And it doesn't give Vensim's numbers: only Euler() with dt = Δt does.","title":"Other solvers: SciML"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Functions-that-count-steps:-the-model's-ticks","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"Most of a model is defined by rates, and any solver integrates them towards the same answer.  Two functions are defined by steps instead:\n\nDELAY FIXED (x(t - τ; init)): the input round(τ / Δt) steps ago.\nSAMPLE IF TRUE (sample_if_true(c, x; init)): at each step, the input if the condition holds, else the value of the step before.\n\nTheir meaning needs a clock: the model's ticks, t₀ + k·Δt.  PySD.jl keeps them as a recording of their inputs at each tick, and whatever the solver:\n\nWhen the solve starts, every tick is added as a stop: the solver lands on each one.\nAt each tick, a callback computes the model there, from the stocks at that moment.  The delays and samples record their inputs and move one step, exactly as after an Euler step.\nBetween two ticks, they are held: a fixed delay gives its input from n ticks before, a sample the value it took at the tick.  The solver's own steps in between only integrate the stocks.\n\nWith Euler() and dt = Δt the ticks are the solver's steps, and the values Vensim's.  With smaller or better steps, the stocks around the delays and samples converge on one answer.  A model with a stock filled through a fixed delay, and a capital that a sample spends from, with Δt = 0.5:\n\nusing PySD, OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK\n\nclock_example = @equations begin\n    @variables begin\n        orders(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n        arrivals(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n        stock(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n        capital(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n        spending(t), [unit = NoUnits]\n    end\n    orders ~ ifelse(t >= 1, 1.0, 0.0)\n    arrivals ~ orders(t - 2; init = 0)                  # DELAY FIXED\n    ∂t(stock) ~ arrivals - stock / 2\n    stock(t₀) ~ 0\n    spending ~ sample_if_true(rem(t, 2) == 0, 0.2 * capital; init = 0)\n    ∂t(capital) ~ 0.25 * capital - spending\n    capital(t₀) ~ 1\nend\nm = Model(clock_example; time = 0:0.5:10, time_unit = NoUnits)\n\nfor (label, r) in ((\"Vensim (Euler, Δt)\", run_model(m)),\n                   (\"Euler, Δt/2\", run_model(m, Euler(); dt = m.dt / 2)),\n                   (\"Euler, Δt/8\", run_model(m, Euler(); dt = m.dt / 8)),\n                   (\"RK4\", run_model(m, RK4())))\n    println(rpad(label, 20), \"stock(3.5) = \", round(r[:stock][8]; digits = 4),\n            \"   capital(10) = \", round(r[:capital][end]; digits = 4))\nend\nVensim (Euler, Δt)  stock(3.5) = 0.5   capital(10) = 1.7652\nEuler, Δt/2         stock(3.5) = 0.4688   capital(10) = 1.8007\nEuler, Δt/8         stock(3.5) = 0.4486   capital(10) = 1.83\nRK4                 stock(3.5) = 0.4424   capital(10) = 1.8403\n\nThe arrivals are the same under every solver: whole ticks, as Vensim.","title":"Functions that count steps: the model's ticks"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#What-Vensim-does-with-Runge-Kutta","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"Vensim's manual says that DELAY FIXED's output does \"not change except at TIME STEP intervals\", \"regardless of the integration technique used\": what PySD.jl does.  For SAMPLE IF TRUE it says that with methods other than Euler the output \"will change within the integration step\", while \"the reported value will always be computed on an even time step\".  PySD.jl holds it between ticks instead; what Vensim does inside its step isn't documented, so it can't be reproduced.","title":"What Vensim does with Runge-Kutta"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Switches-on-the-time","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"t ≥ 2015, STEP, PULSE and PULSE TRAIN are functions of the time, and any solver evaluates them where it is.  A model that has them also stops at every tick, so a switch at a tick falls between two of the solver's steps. A step that ends at a tick, before the callback has moved to it (Runge-Kutta's last stage), sees the time just before the tick: a switch there changes the rates from that tick on, exactly.  (Without this, RK4 would turn it on a sixth of a step early.)\n\nA switch between two ticks (STEP at 2.3 with Δt = 1) is where it is written. Vensim's Euler switches at the next tick (3).","title":"Switches on the time"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Equations-written-for-steps-of-Δt","page":"Solvers and the model's ticks","text":"Some equations are written for Euler steps of Δt, and may mean something else with another solver:\n\nEquation With Euler steps of Δt With another solver\n∂t(x) ~ (target - x) / Δt x is set to target in one step x follows target with a time constant Δt\n∂t(year_met) ~ ifelse(c, t / Δt, 0) the year c holds is kept depends on how long c holds within a step\ng ~ growth / Δt a growth per step a rate per time\nifelse(t == 2020, …) true for one step true at one instant\nnonnegative = true stocks the outflow is cut to what empties the stock in one step the stock decays towards zero instead\n\nThe ticks can't fix these: they are ordinary equations, and only the modeller knows what was meant.  So any solver other than Euler() with dt = Δt names them in a warning, once per model:\n\n┌ Warning: Equations written for Euler steps of Δt, which may mean something else with this solver\n│ (only Euler() with dt = Δt gives Vensim's values); compare with smaller steps to see whether the\n│ results change:\n│   2 use Δt as a value (a move or a rate per step): year_met, drained\n│   1 test the time for equality (true for one Euler step, at one instant here): year_met\n│   1 are non-negative stocks (their outflow is cut to what empties them in one step): level\n\nNot named, as they mean the same with any solver:\n\nan equation that depends on the time alone (parameters, data series, lookups, and fixed delays or samples of them), such as a finite difference of data, (data(t + Δt) - data(t)) / Δt;\na time t - Δt or t + Δt given to a delay or a data series;\nthe condition of a sample_if_true, which is read at the ticks.\n\nNot every one named changes a result.  Running with smaller steps shows which do: a difference that shrinks as the step halves is integration error; one that stays is a change of meaning.","title":"Equations written for steps of Δt"}]
}
